cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Smart Home Surveillance System: an attack-resilient, formally
  verified ESP32 intrusion alarm
message: >-
  If you use this software or its experimental artifact in your
  research, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Humayun
    given-names: MD
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Sardar
      Vallabhbhai National Institute of Technology, Surat, India
repository-code: https://github.com/mdhumayun7/Smart-Home-Automation
abstract: >-
  An ESP32 intrusion alarm used as the object of study for a single
  research question: on a single-chip Wi-Fi SoC, where the attacked
  radio driver shares the die, the RTOS scheduler and the interrupt
  controller with the safety task, does pinning the safety task to its
  own core actually isolate it? The firmware runs a five-state alarm
  machine on a pinned core, isolated from Wi-Fi and MQTT by bounded
  queues and a non-blocking status snapshot, and adds HMAC-SHA256
  command authentication over an NVS-persisted monotonic counter,
  passive 802.11 deauthentication detection, a signed ESP-NOW fallback
  alert channel and optional PIR plus mmWave sensor fusion. The
  repository is a reproducible artifact: two TLA+ specifications
  checked exhaustively by TLC and mutation-tested, a seeded 6,000-trial
  adversarial campaign, a 3,000,000-iteration sanitizer-backed fuzzing
  campaign, and a specified measurement protocol for the one claim that
  requires physical hardware, which remains open.
keywords:
  - embedded systems
  - ESP32
  - FreeRTOS
  - real-time systems
  - safety-critical systems
  - IoT security
  - formal verification
  - TLA+
  - model checking
  - fuzzing
  - MQTT
  - home automation
